WEBVTT

NOTE Narration by an AI voice: Matilda (XrExE9yKIg1WjnnlVkGX), eleven_v4.

intro
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:08.700
Volt Typhoon dumps LSASS with a built-in Windows DLL. Copilot checks our coverage and ships a fix as code.

ask
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:16.520
The engineer asks whether Enterprise Security would catch it, and says not to change anything.

read
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:24.860
Copilot reads the deployed detections through Splunk MCP, with their searches and the macros behind them.

disabled
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:34.200
ESCU has a rule for this, but it's off, and its search needs MiniDump or ordinal twenty-four.

miss
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:42.820
Call the same function by another ordinal, or leave off the DLL extension, and that rule stays silent.

repo
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:49.320
Copilot builds the fix in the team's detections repo, following its agents file.

sigma
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:56.260
It starts from SigmaHQ's community rule, which already covers the ordinal tricks.

tests
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:04.520
Real attack data from Splunk's threat research team becomes a test that must fire, plus crafted variants.

build
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:15.120
sigma-cli converts the rule for the CIM endpoint data model, and contentctl builds an Enterprise Security correlation search.

nothing
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.500
Nothing is pushed yet, and nothing in Splunk has changed.

pr
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.560
Copilot opens a pull request, and GitHub Actions takes over.

workflow
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.680
At right, the validate workflow runs two jobs on GitHub-hosted runners.

build-job
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:38.600
Build lints the Sigma rule, checks the search still matches it, and packages the app with contentctl.

test-job
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:49.380
Test sends each case to Splunk over HEC, runs our search beside ESCU's, and counts a week of real alerts.

compare
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:56.180
ESCU's rule misses all three ordinal variants. Ours catches each one.

noise
00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:02.660
But a week of real endpoint data raises sixteen alerts, over the budget of five.

triage
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:10.120
Splunk AI Toolkit triages them: a purple-team account on a lab host, testing nightly.

report
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:15.700
CI fails, and posts its report on the pull request, at right.

precedent
00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:21.620
Copilot sees the team already excludes that run, and asks before tuning.

tune
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:29.400
Copilot adds the same narrow exclusion: that account and that host together, nothing broader.

silent-case
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.880
It adds that run as a test that must stay silent.

push
00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:37.740
Copilot pushes, and validate runs again.

green-run
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:43.000
Both jobs pass at right, in under two and a half minutes.

green
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.600
Twelve of twelve cases pass, and noise drops from sixteen to zero.

diff
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.880
The tuning lives in one macro, so the diff shows what is excluded.

git
00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.360
Every change is in Git, reviewed and tested before production.

person
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.920
Switched on, ESCU's rule would page the SOC about it nightly.

gate-why
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:11.300
Merging deploys, so the gate holds it for your approval.

approved
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:20.200
Approved. Copilot merges, and the deploy workflow installs the detection over the REST API.

prune
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:26.120
It also removes detections no longer in the repo, so Splunk matches Git.

deploy-run
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:35.240
At right, one deploy job rebuilds the app, installs it, and checks every detection is on and scheduled.

check
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:41.080
Then Copilot checks the live result in Splunk, not just the pipeline.

live
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:47.800
Splunk MCP confirms it's on, hourly, and a correlation search.

fires
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.760
Run once, it finds the replayed attack and every variant.

content
00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.800
Content management shows it live beside ESCU's rule, still off.

spl
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:05.040
This is the search Enterprise Security now runs, generated from the Sigma rule.

tstats
00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:12.920
It uses tstats on the CIM Endpoint data model, so it reads accelerated summaries, not raw events.

logic
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:23.320
It flags rundll32 loading comsvcs with the full-dump flag, whether the call names MiniDump or any form of ordinal twenty-four.

macro
00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:29.620
The last macro is the team's tuning, kept apart so the logic stays Sigma's.

agentic
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.480
Agentic workflows with Splunk MCP and AI Toolkit turned ESCU's default rule into a stronger detection.

practice
00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:49.700
And the detection-as-code practices held: it's in Git, tested on real attacks, tuned with AI triage, and shipped by CI after your approval.
